<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>Helix Dynamics — Exit Interview</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9010-whistleblower.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the player contacted the Interstellar Engineering Standards Bureau directly with evidence of the prior Lab 7 incident after being terminated?" />
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9006-termination-aftermath-quiet-corridor-reflection</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      HR Suite, Ring 3. Beige. A plant that might be artificial. A window
      that looks into a corridor rather than out into space. The HR
      representative has a very neutral face and is very good at her job.
      Your personal locker has already been cleared. Security Officer
      Oluwaseun is waiting in the corridor — not unkindly, just
      procedurally. Your station badge has been deactivated.
      You can feel that it's deactivated because the door to
      the suite opened for her, not for you.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      Termination of a probationary contract at Helix Dynamics is routine
      and processed within 24 hours. You will receive your first month's
      pay, a standard reference that confirms dates of employment and
      job title, and a document that you are not required to sign but are
      strongly encouraged to: a non-disclosure agreement with a
      17,000-credit settlement offer. The HR representative explains
      this calmly. It is described as "customary."
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      HR Suite. A table. Two chairs. A terminal. A box with your things
      in it — your mug, your personal tablet, a half-finished pack of
      the coffee you brought from home. You have one hour before the
      shuttle to the transit hub departs. After that, no scheduled
      departures for sixteen hours.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      You were found in Lab 7 at 23:47 on your first day of employment.
      Security Officer Oluwaseun was professional throughout. The Category 3
      access violation was logged. Your probationary contract was terminated
      at 08:00 the following morning. You have not yet decided whether
      to sign the NDA. The fragment of test printout you found is in
      your jacket pocket — you palmed it before Oluwaseun could see it.
      You haven't been asked about it. You don't know if they know
      you have it. You have about forty minutes before you have
      to decide about the shuttle.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      HR Representative Soo-Jin Park: Efficient, neutral, not unkind. She has
      done this many times. She is not the enemy. She is the process. She will
      answer direct questions with whatever she is permitted to tell you —
      which is not much, but is not nothing.

      Tomás Engel: Messaged you at 07:47: "heard what happened. i'm sorry.
      you okay?" He does not know about the printout. He is still inside,
      still employed, starting on his team assignment today.

      Chief Engineer Ryo Sato: You've never met him. His name is on the
      Lab 7 test log you saw. He knows that. Whether he knows about the
      printout in your pocket is the question you cannot answer.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>HR Suite, Ring 3, Helix Dynamics Orbital Yard</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        Terminated on day one. In possession of an unofficial document
        that may indicate a suppressed incident on the program you were
        hired to work on. NDA on the table. Shuttle in forty minutes.
        Badge deactivated.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        This is not how today was supposed to go. But you have something
        in your pocket, and a decision to make, and the clock is running.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      This is not a failure state — it is a divergent path. The player
      made an audacious choice, got caught, and now faces a different
      and genuinely interesting situation. The NDA is real moral pressure,
      not a cartoon villain move: it's a lot of money, and the player
      was terminated fairly. The printout is ambiguous evidence —
      suggestive, not conclusive. The tone is quiet, a little defeated,
      but not hopeless. The player still has agency. The most interesting
      choice in the room is the one they haven't made yet.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
